### Description:
This Terraform program sets up a non-alias AWS Route 53 record that points to an AWS Route 53 zone named "internal.example53.com". The zone is associated with an AWS VPC named "main".

### Terraform HCL Program:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"  # Specify your desired AWS region
}

variable "zone_name" {
  default = "internal.example53.com"
}

variable "vpc_id" {
  default = "vpc-1234567890abcdef0"  # Replace with your actual VPC ID
}

resource "aws_route53_zone" "private_zone" {
  name = var.zone_name
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "example_record" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.private_zone.id
  name     = "example.internal.example53.com"
  type    = "A"
  ttl      = 300

  alias {
    evaluate_target_health = false
    name                  = aws_vpc.main.dns_name
    zone_id               = aws_vpc.main.vpc_id
  }
}
```

### Explanation:
- **Provider Configuration**: The `provider "aws"` block specifies the AWS region where the resources will be created. You can change this to any valid AWS region.
- **Variables**: 
  - `zone_name`: A variable for the name of the Route 53 zone. It defaults to "internal.example53.com".
  - `vpc_id`: A variable for the ID of the VPC. You need to replace `"vpc-1234567890abcdef0"` with your actual VPC ID.
- **Route 53 Zone**: The `aws_route53_zone` resource creates a new Route 53 zone with the specified name.
- **Route 53 Record**: The `aws_route53_record` resource creates an A record that points to the DNS name of the VPC. The `alias` block is used to create a non-alias record, which means the record will not be updated automatically if the VPC's DNS name changes.

This configuration is deployable with Terraform ~> 1.9.8 and hashicorp/aws ~> 5.75. Make sure to replace placeholders like `vpc-1234567890abcdef0` with your actual AWS resource IDs.

